course: books
lesson: 39
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IV, part 3: Mr. Badger (the Otter comes through the snow, and the best home is underground)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The front-door bell clangs, and in bursts the Otter, hugging the Rat so hard he splutters through his buttered toast. All the river bank was in a panic, he says — Rat and Mole gone all night! — but he guessed they'd be at Badger's, because anyone in trouble ends up at Badger's.
explain: He tells of his walk through the snowy wood at sunrise: snow-castles, snow sliding off branches with a «flop!», robins looking proud as if they had done it all themselves. He met a silly rabbit who said all the rabbits knew the Mole was being chased last night — and none of them did a thing to help. So the Otter cuffed him and left him.
explain: The Otter asks the Mole to fry him some ham, and the Mole sets the hedgehogs to do it. While they fry it, the Otter and the Rat talk river talk without end, and at last the Badger comes in, yawning and rubbing his eyes. He asks the Otter to lunch and sends the little hedgehogs home to their mother, with sixpence each and a pat on the head.
explain: At lunch the Mole tells the Badger how lovely it is to live underground: nothing can get at you, and you are your own master. The Badger beams — that's exactly what he thinks! Floods chase the Rat out of his house, and a big house like Toad's can burn or leak; but underground there is no weather at all. «That's my idea of home!»
fact: The front-door bell «clanged loudly»; the Rat, «very greasy with buttered toast», sends Billy, the smaller hedgehog, to open it.
fact: The Otter «threw himself on the Rat with an embrace and a shout of affectionate greeting». «Get off!» splutters the Rat, with his mouth full.
fact: Along the River Bank they were «in a great state of alarm»: «Rat never been home all night—nor Mole either», and the snow had covered their tracks.
fact: The Otter knew «when people were in any fix (= trouble) they mostly went to Badger, or else Badger got to know of it somehow».
fact: In the snowy wood: snow slid off branches «with a flop!», there were «snow-castles and snow-caverns», and «robins perched and hopped on them in their perky conceited way, just as if they had done it themselves». (Conceited = too proud of yourself.)
fact: He met a rabbit «cleaning his silly face with his paws», and «had to cuff his head once or twice» (cuff = hit with an open paw) to get any sense out of him.
fact: The rabbit said it was «the talk of the burrows» that the Mole was lost and «‘They’ were up and out hunting, and were chivvying him round and round» (chivvy = chase and harass).
fact: The Otter: «there are hundreds and hundreds of you … you could have taken him in». The rabbit: «‘What, us? do something? us rabbits?’»
fact: Asked if he wasn't nervous, the Otter laughs, showing «a gleaming set of strong white teeth»: «I'd give 'em nerves if any of them tried anything on with me.»
fact: The Otter and the Rat «talked river-shop, which is long shop and talk that is endless, running on like the babbling river itself». (Talking shop = talking about your own work or hobby.)
fact: At lunch-time the two hedgehogs, hungry again after their porridge, looked up at the Badger without speaking, too shy to say so.
fact: The Badger gives the hedgehogs «sixpence apiece and a pat on the head»; they leave «with much respectful swinging of caps and touching of forelocks» (the old polite way of greeting your «betters» — today we don't sort people into betters and lesser folk).
fact: The Mole: «Once well underground … you know exactly where you are. Nothing can happen to you, and nothing can get at you.»
fact: The Badger: «There's no security, or peace and tranquillity, except underground … No builders, no tradesmen, no remarks passed on you by fellows looking over your wall, and, above all, no weather.» If you need more room, «a dig and a scrape» and you have it; if a hole lets in trouble, you stop it up.
fact: The Badger on the Rat: when the floods come he has to move out into hired lodgings, «uncomfortable, inconveniently situated».
fact: About Toad Hall: «quite the best house in these parts, as a house. But supposing a fire breaks out—where's Toad?» And: «I hate a draught myself.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bussin'» = really tasty; «mardy» (Midlands, North England) = sulky, moody; «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon; «wicked» (New England, US) = very; «sus» = suspicious, dodgy.
exercise: Who sends Billy to open the front door? | The Rat, greasy with buttered toast | The Mole, busy warming the coffee-pot | The Badger, from his study | Nobody — Billy goes by himself because he is curious
exercise: How does the Otter greet the Rat? | With a big hug and a shout | With a cold, stiff bow from the doorway, and no smile at all | By pushing him off his chair into the fire | With a letter
exercise: What does the Rat say when the Otter hugs him? | «Get off!» | «How lovely to see you, old friend, come and sit down» | «Who are you, sir?» | «Help, a bear!»
exercise: Why was everyone on the River Bank alarmed? | Rat and Mole had not been home all night | Toad had smashed another car on the bridge that morning | The river had frozen solid | The weasels had moved to the river
exercise: Why couldn't the river animals follow Rat and Mole's tracks? | The snow had covered them up | The weasels had swept them away with their tails | Nobody thought of looking | The tracks went into the river
exercise: How did the Otter guess where they were? | Anyone in a fix goes to Badger | A bird told him the whole story at dawn | He followed their tracks | He heard the Badger's bell
exercise: When did the Otter come through the Wild Wood? | As the red sun was rising | At midnight, by the light of the moon | In the warm afternoon | He came round it, along the river
exercise: Which birds does the Otter call «perky» and «conceited»? | The robins | The wild geese flying overhead in a ragged line | The rooks in the elms | The ducks on the pond
exercise: Why do the robins look «conceited»? | As if they had broken the branches themselves | Because they sing better than any other bird | Because they have red waistcoats | They don't — the Otter says they look sad
exercise: What does the Otter say he «could have stayed and played with for hours»? | Snow-castles, caverns, bridges and terraces | The rabbits | The robins | The frozen pond in the middle of the wood
exercise: What was the rabbit doing when the Otter found him? | Sitting on a stump, cleaning his silly face | Running for his life through the trees, shouting | Digging a new burrow | Asleep in the snow
exercise: What news did the Otter get out of the rabbit? | Mole had been seen lost, and «They» were hunting him | Mole had fallen into the river near the weir | Toad had driven through the wood | Badger was ill
exercise: What did the Otter ask the rabbit? | Why didn't any of you do something? | Where does the Badger live, exactly, and which is his door? | Have you seen my son? | Can I have some carrots?
exercise: What did the rabbit answer? | «What, us? do something? us rabbits?» | «We did help — we hid him in our burrow all night» | «We were asleep» | «The Badger told us not to»
exercise: «I'd give 'em nerves if any of them tried anything on with me.» Who does the Otter mean by «'em»? | The Wild Wood animals who hunt | The rabbits, because they wouldn't help | The hedgehogs | The Badger and the Rat
exercise: What does the Otter ask the Mole to do? | Fry him some ham | Run home and tell the river bank | Fetch the Badger from his study | Make a snowman in the yard
exercise: Who actually fries the ham? | The hedgehogs | The Mole himself, very carefully | The Otter, still in his coat | The Rat, with buttered fingers
exercise: «River-shop» talk is… | Long, endless talk about river things | Talk about buying and selling boats in a shop by the river | A secret language | Shouting
exercise: How does the Badger come into the kitchen? | Yawning and rubbing his eyes | Running, with a lantern and a cudgel, ready for a fight | Carrying a pile of books from his study | Singing
exercise: What does the Badger give the hedgehogs before they go home? | Sixpence each and a pat on the head | A basket of eggs and a jar of honey for their mother | A lantern each to light their way home | A ride home on his broad back through the snow
exercise: Why do the hedgehogs look up at Badger without speaking? | They are hungry again, but too shy to say so | They are afraid he will be cross about the ham | They want to stay for the night | They can't talk
exercise: What does the Mole say is best about living underground? | You know where you are, and nothing can get at you | You can see the stars through the roof | It is always light | You have lots of visitors
exercise: How does the Badger react to the Mole's words? | He beams on him — that's just what he says | He laughs and says the Mole is wrong | He falls asleep | He gets cross
exercise: What does the Badger say there is «no» of underground, «above all»? | Weather | Food in winter | Friends calling | Noise or light
exercise: What happens to the Rat, says the Badger, when the river floods? | He must move into uncomfortable hired lodgings | He goes to live with Toad at the Hall | He swims about happily | He sleeps on the roof
exercise: What does the Badger say about Toad Hall? | The best house in these parts, as a house — but… | It is a terrible, ugly house and should be pulled down | It is underground | It is too small for Toad
exercise: Which of these does the Badger say he hates? | A draught | Visitors in winter | Snow on the doorstep | Toad and his cars
test: Ethan: «the Otter rocks up with his tracking skills and follows the footprints.» (rock up = arrive) Put it right. | Snow covered the tracks; he just guessed they'd be at Badger's | Right: he sniffed out every single step they took through the wood | He came by boat | The Badger sent for him
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The rabbits hid the Mole in their burrows when the weasels chased him.» | False: they knew, but did nothing | True: hundreds of rabbits took him in and kept him warm | True, the Otter thanks them | False: the rabbits chased him
test: «That rabbit's well mardy, he won't help nobody.» (mardy = sulky) What is the Otter really angry about? | There were hundreds of rabbits, and none of them tried to help | The rabbit wouldn't give him a carrot from his big store under the stump | The rabbit was rude about the river | The rabbit stole his hat
test: Kai: «wicked cool, the Otter's walk.» (wicked = very, in New England) Which of these was NOT in the Otter's story of his walk? | A frozen waterfall that he slid down | Snow sliding off branches with a flop | Robins hopping proudly | Snow-castles and snow-caverns
test: Looking back: in chapter III, a rabbit ran past the Mole in the Wild Wood. What did he mutter? | «Get out of this, you fool, get out!» | «Follow me, I'll show you a safe burrow» | «Onion-sauce!» | «Good evening, Mr. Mole»
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat say about rabbits? | «Some of 'em, but rabbits are a mixed lot» | «Rabbits are the bravest animals in the whole Wild Wood» | «Never speak to rabbits» | «Rabbits are all thieves»
test: Looking back: in chapter I, an old rabbit asked the Mole for sixpence to pass. What did the Mole do? | Bowled him over and shouted «Onion-sauce!» | Paid the sixpence politely and went through | Went the long way round | Cried
test: Looking back: how did the Otter disappear at the picnic in chapter I? | He dived after a May-fly — «cloop!» — and was gone | He said goodbye and walked away along the bank | He was chased off by Toad | He fell asleep
test: Looking back: what were the Otter's first words at the picnic in chapter I? | «Greedy beggars! Why didn't you invite me, Ratty?» | «Proud, I'm sure» | «Hullo, Mole!» | «Such a rumpus everywhere, I'm going home»
test: Looking back: why did the Mole leave his own underground home in chapter I? | Spring called him up out of his spring-cleaning | A flood washed him out | The weasels chased him | Rat invited him to stay
test: Mole now says underground is the best place. Does that fit what he did in chapter I? | Partly — he left his hole for the sunshine, yet he still loves being underground | Yes: he never wanted to come up at all, and the Rat dragged him out of his hole by force | No, he hates being underground | Yes, he moved back home in chapter I
test: «Supposing a fire breaks out—where's Toad?» What does the Badger mean? | A big house above ground can burn or fall apart, and Toad can't fix that | Toad always runs away from fires and leaves his friends and servants to put them out | Toad has lost his way | Toad is hiding in the cellar
test: What would happen to the Badger if a flood came? | Not much — he could stop up a hole or dig a new room | He would have to move into hired lodgings, like the Rat | He would drown | He would go to Toad Hall
test: Freya from Melbourne: «the Badger reckons a house should have heaps of windows and a view, this arvo he said it.» (heaps = lots) Put it right. | He says underground is best: no weather, no one looking over your wall | Right: he loves windows and wants to build a big glass house | He wants to live by the river | He says Toad Hall is perfect
test: ★★★ «There's no security, or peace and tranquillity, except underground.» Who in this book so far would most DISAGREE? | The Rat, who loves his breezy river home | The Mole, who has just said almost the same thing | The hedgehogs | The Badger himself
test: ★★★ The Otter «had to cuff his head once or twice». Count all the times he hits the rabbit in his story, if «once or twice» means at most two. | At most three | Exactly one | Exactly five | None
test: ★★★ «In the stillness, every now and then masses of snow slid off the branches.» Which word in this lesson's story sounds just like what it names? | Flop | Stillness | Masses | Branches
exam: Identify the principal ground upon which the Otter concluded that the missing animals were to be found at the Badger's residence. | The general habit of animals in difficulty to resort to the Badger | The discovery of their footprints in the snow, leading to his door | Information supplied by the hedgehogs | A message from the Badger
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reply of the rabbit to the Otter's reproach? | Astonishment that rabbits should be expected to act | An assurance that assistance had been rendered to the Mole during the night | A refusal to speak | An apology
exam: It is given that the Badger dismissed the hedgehogs. Ascertain the provision made for them. | Sixpence apiece and a pat on the head | A meal of fried ham and a basket of provisions for their mother | Nothing whatsoever | A lantern
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Badger's principal objection to dwellings above ground. | Their exposure to weather and to accident | Their cost of construction, which he held to be excessive and wasteful | Their distance from the river | Their want of cellars
